            <ArticleTitle>The influence of personality on reported pain and self-efficacy for pain management in older cancer patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1261-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1359105313488976</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This study examines the relationship of personality traits and affect on cancer-related pain in 150 older adults receiving outpatient treatment at a comprehensive cancer center. Regression analyses revealed extraversion as a significant predictor of current pain, with openness to experience as a significant indicator of average pain. Similarly, positive affect and negative affect were significant predictors of self-efficacy for pain management. Moderation models showed that conscientiousness and extraversion were significant moderators in the relationship between self-efficacy for pain management and worst pain. These findings suggest that different personality types may influence perceptions of pain severity.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© The Author(s) 2013.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
